---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Two = `CROWDSOURCING

  the practice of obtaining input or information
  from a large number of people via the Internet

CITIZEN SCIENCE

  scientific research conducted in whole or part by
  distributed individuals, many of whom may not be
  scientists, who contribute relevant data to
  research using their own computing devices

Citizen science is a KIND of crowdsourcing,
aimed at research.`;

const Enables = `WIDESPREAD ACCESS TO INFORMATION AND
PUBLIC DATA FACILITATES

  identifying problems
  developing solutions
  disseminating results

All three. Not just "finding things out."`;

const Funding = `A NEW MODEL FOR COLLABORATION

  "Crowdsourcing offers new models for
   collaboration, such as connecting businesses
   or social causes with funding."

Many people, small contributions, no institution
in the middle deciding what deserves money.`;

const Suited = `WHAT CITIZEN SCIENCE IS GOOD FOR

  counting fireflies across a whole coastline
    lots of people, no training, cheap equipment

WHAT IT IS NOT GOOD FOR

  testing a vaccine on volunteers
    needs a trained professional, controlled
    conditions, and accountability

The question is not "are these people clever."
It is what the task actually requires.`;

const Accuracy = `THE COST

  Anyone can contribute -- so anyone can contribute
  something wrong.

  And with the ease of posting a legitimate
  crowdfunding cause comes the ease of posting
  a false one.

Openness is the feature. It is also the hole.`;

const Skill = `THE SKILL FOR THIS TOPIC IS 1.C

  "Explain how collaboration affects the
   development of a solution."

That is a Big Idea 1 skill, assessed here.
You met it at session 53.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.14: Crowdsourcing and Citizen Science</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Optimistic Session</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Two sessions of harms and one of unequal access.</b> This one is about
            something computing made possible that was not possible before: getting
            useful work from a very large number of people who are not organized into
            anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">It has a cost too</b>, and the cost is the same
            property that makes it work.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.14</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Crowdsourcing and Citizen Science</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Two Related Definitions</p>
              <p>What the Crowd Is Good For</p>
              <p>Openness Cuts Both Ways</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Definitions</h2>
              <Code code={Two} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Both Are Examinable" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Detail People Drop</h2>
              <p>Citizen science: <b>using their own computing devices.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Not a lab's equipment. Their phone, their laptop, their spare processor time.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Which is what makes it possible at all &mdash; and what limits what it can measure.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Things It Enables</h2>
              <Code code={Enables} frame="code" lang="txt" title="IOC-1.E.1" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Identifying</b> a problem is on that list, before solving one.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Sometimes the Crowd Finds the Problem</h2>
              <p>Not just the answer. <b>The question.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Thousands of people noticing the same thing is itself a finding, and
                no institution had to commission it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Money, Too</h2>
              <Code code={Funding} frame="code" lang="txt" title="A New Model" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">A community garden gets funded by people who walk past it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What Is It Actually Good For?</h2>
              <Code code={Suited} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Fit the Task" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Test</h2>
              <p><b>Does the task need many observers, or does it need trained ones?</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Many, cheap, spread out, low skill &rarr; the crowd is ideal.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Few, expensive, controlled, accountable &rarr; the crowd is the wrong tool.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Human Capability, Enhanced</h2>
              <p><b>&quot;Human capabilities can be enhanced by collaboration via computing.&quot;</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A hundred thousand people counting fireflies is not a hundred thousand
                small results.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>It is one result nobody could have produced alone.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And the Cost</h2>
              <Code code={Accuracy} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Same Property" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">You cannot have open contribution and vetted contribution.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which You Have Seen Before</h2>
              <p>Open protocols let the internet grow, and let anyone connect. <b>Session 66.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Crowdsourcing lets anyone contribute, including badly.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Openness is a trade, not a free gift.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>One Thing You Owe the Crowd</h2>
              <p>If you use something the crowd gave you, <b>credit it.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                You did this on the Create Task. <b>Session 74 is why it is a rule.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Skill Being Assessed</h2>
              <Code code={Skill} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Not What You Expect" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Defining crowdsourcing and citizen science, and relating them</li>
                <li>Naming what widespread access to public data enables</li>
                <li>Judging whether a task suits a crowd</li>
                <li>Explaining how crowdsourcing connects causes with funding</li>
                <li>Explaining how collaboration affects the development of a solution</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Crowdsourcing</dt>
                  <dd>Obtaining input or information from a large number of people via the internet.</dd>
                  <dt>Citizen science</dt>
                  <dd>Research conducted in whole or part by distributed individuals, many not scientists, contributing data using their own computing devices.</dd>
                  <dt>Dissemination</dt>
                  <dd>Spreading results. The third thing public data access makes easier.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Which Is It?</h3>

          <p>
            For each: <b>crowdsourcing</b>, <b>citizen science</b>, <b>both</b>, or{" "}
            <b>neither</b>. One reason each.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Volunteers photograph and log every bird they see in one county</li>
            <li>A map app asks drivers to report road closures</li>
            <li>A company pays a research firm to survey a thousand shoppers</li>
            <li>People donate spare processor time to help analyze telescope data</li>
            <li>A site collects small donations to fund a community garden</li>
            <li>Volunteers transcribe handwritten historical weather records</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Item 3 is the one to be careful with.</b> Say what makes it different
            from the others.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Suited, or Not</h3>

          <p>For each task, say whether a crowd could do it and <b>what the task actually requires</b>.</p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Crowd?</th><th>What it requires</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Counting fireflies along a coastline</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Testing a new medicine on volunteers</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Spotting flooded roads after a storm</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Diagnosing a specific patient's illness</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Transcribing a million handwritten records</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Deciding a prison sentence</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            <b>The last row is not a scientific task at all.</b> Say why it belongs on
            this list anyway.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Design One</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Design a citizen science project for your own area.</b> Something a crowd
            could genuinely do.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>The question</b> you are trying to answer. One sentence.</li>
            <li><b>What each contributor does</b>, and roughly how long it takes them.</li>
            <li><b>What device</b> they use. It must be one they already own.</li>
            <li><b>Why a crowd</b> rather than three trained researchers.</li>
            <li><b>How the data could be wrong</b>, and one thing you would do about it.</li>
            <li><b>How you would disseminate the result.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Question 5 is the one that separates a real design
            from a nice idea.</b> Every crowdsourced dataset has bad entries in it &mdash;
            you met that at session 44.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: The Same Property, Twice</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Openness makes crowdsourcing work and makes it exploitable.</b>
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Describe <b>one benefit</b> that exists <i>only because</i> anyone can
              contribute.
            </li>
            <li>
              Describe <b>one harm</b> that exists <i>only because</i> anyone can
              contribute.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Propose a rule</b> that would reduce the harm. <b>Then say what benefit
              your rule also reduces.</b>
            </li>
            <li>
              Session 66 said the internet's protocols are <b>open</b>, and that this is
              why it could grow. <b>Describe what these two topics have in common.</b>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Define crowdsourcing.</li>
            <li>Define citizen science, including <b>whose devices</b> are used.</li>
            <li>Name the three things widespread access to public data facilitates.</li>
            <li>Give one task a crowd suits and one it does not, with the reason.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>&quot;Human capabilities can be enhanced by collaboration via computing.&quot; Describe a result your Task 3 project could produce that no single person could produce alone, however hard they worked.</li>
            <li>Crowdsourced data is vast and often not technical. Explain why that is a reasonable trade for some questions and a disqualifying problem for others.</li>
            <li>You had to credit any code you did not write on the Create Task. Explain what that rule has in common with crowdsourcing.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tasks, including your project design, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
